00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:18.780
so what is rosacea?

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:24.840
H: rosacea is a chronic inflammatory&nbsp;
skin condition it's basically my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:28.080
dermatologist explained it to me as it's the blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:31.920
vessels that are inflamed not the&nbsp;
pores like you would have in acne

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:37.800
you'll see visible blood vessels so those&nbsp;
little red ones are called telangiectasias&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:44.520
redness flushing so you'll see a symmetrical&nbsp;
pattern of redness so mine comes and goes

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:47.820
so you might get sort of a pimple like bump but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:53.160
won't be in the t-zone it will be around&nbsp;
the cheeks and the nose in this pattern

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:55.950
so it's like w zone not t-zone

00:13:55.950 --> 00:14:01.470
M: it gets confused a lot with acne and it&nbsp;
tends to show up from your late 20s onwards

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:04.440
M: so how did you first&nbsp;
suspect that you had rosacea?

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:09.420
H: I was at an event and Dr Dennis&nbsp;
was there and I said "you know what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:13.260
I'm always like flushing pink and red&nbsp;
through here sometimes through my cheeks

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:16.080
what's going on? is that something I can treat?"

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:21.540
he's like "well that's rosacea" and I&nbsp;
was like "see ya" I didn't want to know

00:14:21.540 --> 00:14:24.000
M: why should you actually&nbsp;
look into managing rosacea?

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:27.720
H: the thing about rosacea is that it progresses

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:32.220
so you'll start with just the pink&nbsp;
and the flushing and maybe some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:35.580
visible blood vessels around the nose&nbsp;
you can get them in your cheeks too

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:40.320
and then you'll start to get lesions&nbsp;
and you can also have it get into your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:45.120
eyes and then it can progress&nbsp;
to thickened skin and swelling

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:48.660
M: in terms of treating rosacea there&nbsp;
are over-the-counter options like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:51.960
azelaic acid you should also&nbsp;
see a dermatologist who has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:54.840
experience with treating rosacea and&nbsp;
they'll have other options available

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:56.940
how else do you manage rosacea?

00:14:56.940 --> 00:15:00.660
H: Keeping on top of your skin barrier&nbsp;
and keeping that in check in place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:03.420
really supporting it is number one

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:07.860
And avoiding triggers so you'll notice&nbsp;
when you start to flush from certain things

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:09.360
the sun is a big one

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:13.020
my skin hates the indoor heating - hates it

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:17.640
alcohol some foods even spicy food is a big one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:21.420
especially for like pale people&nbsp;
that only eat meat and vegetables

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:24.960
some medicines even do it your&nbsp;
skin care or makeup could do it

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:30.180
M: I feel like in the skincare community we&nbsp;
talk so much about acne but nowhere near as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:34.620
much about rosacea so if any of this is&nbsp;
sounding familiar then you might want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:37.800
to read up a bit on rosacea and&nbsp;
consider getting it checked out

